{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#11",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "text": "ObsAwareData methods > 3. Reliability flag rules > 3.4 NWSS wastewater\n- **Category jump.** When `wastewater_activity_category` moves three or more tiers (on the scale Very Low < Low < Moderate < High < Very High; for example Very Low to High) from the site's previous reported week in the same source release, that week's category and activity level are both `flagged_anomalous`. A two-tier threshold would flag about 14% of all site-weeks, mostly ordinary swings around a category boundary and real rises at the start of a season; three tiers flags about 5%.\n- **Blended source.** Weeks where the source release reports a blended data source for the site are `flagged_anomalous` for both category and level. These are typically one-off laboratory or pipeline transitions."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#24",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "text": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 6. Reliability flags\n| Code | Meaning | Where it occurs |\n|---|---|---|\n| `flagged_anomalous` | Reported, but an unusually large change from the previous week, or (wastewater) a week where CDC blended data from more than one source. Worth checking before use. | `hospital_admission_count`, `ed_visit_percent_smoothed`, wastewater level and category |\n| `incidental_missing` | No value reported for a week within an otherwise active series. The value cell is blank. | `hospital_admission_count`, both ED percents |\n| `structural_absent` | Not collected in this period by design. Currently only RSV hospital data up to 2024-08-03, before RSV reporting was required. | NHSN RSV measures |"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#15",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "text": "ObsAwareData methods > 4. How the thresholds were set\n**Wastewater.** The wastewater target shares (22.6%, 21.4%, 19.2%) are the shares originally intended for South Carolina. South Carolina's own wastewater cutoffs are derived with the same percentile method as every other state, which gives 0.75, 0.76, and 0.76.\n\n**Ties.** Many sites share the same coverage ratio (for example, sites that joined on the same date and missed the same weeks). A site is flagged only when its ratio is strictly below the threshold, so sites tied at the threshold are not flagged. The share actually flagged can therefore fall below the target. In South Carolina, 22.6% of COVID-19, 14.3% of influenza, and 11.5% of RSV site-pathogen pairs are flagged.\n\nThe values applied to each state are in `metadata/observation_state_thresholds.csv` and in each state's quality report."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#6",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "text": "ObsAwareData data sources > 3. Wastewater: NWSS\n- **Dataset:** CDC Wastewater Viral Activity Level for SARS-CoV-2, Influenza A and RSV.\n- **Publisher:** CDC, National Wastewater Surveillance System (NWSS).\n- **Link:** [atcp-73re](https://data.cdc.gov/Public-Health-Surveillance/CDC-Wastewater-Viral-Activity-Level-for-SARS-CoV-2/atcp-73re)\n- **Releases used:** <!-- auto:releases_nwss -->11<!-- /auto:releases_nwss --> dated downloads, the latest dated <!-- auto:newest_nwss -->2026-10-16<!-- /auto:newest_nwss -->.\n- **Fields used:**\n  - Site-level Wastewater Viral Activity Level (WVAL) and its category\n  - The date each site was included in WVAL reporting\n  - The reported data source, used only for a quality flag"
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_methods#16",
      "doc_id": "oad_methods",
      "text": "ObsAwareData methods > 5. Known limitations\n1. **The flag thresholds are provisional.** They are data-derived and have not been externally or clinically validated. Treat the flags as screening aids, not ground truth.\n2. **Coverage flags are static for ED and wastewater.** The NSSP participation status reflects a single August 2026 facility map. The NWSS participation status is one summary per site and pathogen in each source release. Neither describes how coverage changed over time.\n3. **Some region IDs repeat across region types.** `analysis_region_id` is unique only together with `analysis_region_type`. In Pennsylvania, the HSA with code `42` and the state FIPS code `42` coexist.\n4. **Source coverage varies by state and period.**\n   - NSSP data begin in October 2022.\n   - The NSSP `combined` series ends in September 2024 in most states.\n   - NHSN influenza reporting in most states begins in October 2020.\n   - North Dakota has no influenza A or RSV wastewater data. Its COVID-19 wastewater data (2 sites) end on 2023-09-16."
    }
  ]
}

